. What is a Queue?

A Queue is a linear data structure in which insertion and deletion take place at different ends.

It follows the principle:

FIFO — First In, First Out

The element that is inserted first is removed first.

Example
Front                         Rear
  ↓                            ↓
[10] → [20] → [30] → [40]
10 entered first.
Therefore, 10 will leave first.
New elements are added from the rear.
Elements are removed from the front.
2. FIFO Principle

FIFO stands for:

First In, First Out

Example:

Enqueue: 10 → 20 → 30 → 40

Dequeue order:

10 → 20 → 30 → 40

The first element inserted is the first one removed.

Real-life example

Imagine students standing in a line for a ticket:

Student A → Student B → Student C → Student D

Student A came first, so A gets served first.

This is exactly how a Queue works.

3. Important Terminology

A Queue has two important ends:

Front

The front points to the element that will be removed next.

Rear

The rear points to the position where a new element is inserted.

             Queue

Front                           Rear
 ↓                               ↓
[10] → [20] → [30] → [40]

Remember:

Insertion → Rear
Deletion → Front

4. Basic Operations of Queue

There are five important operations.

4.1 Enqueue

Enqueue means inserting an element into the queue.

Before:

[10] → [20] → [30]

Enqueue 40

After:

[10] → [20] → [30] → [40]

Insertion takes place at the rear.

4.2 Dequeue

Dequeue means removing an element from the queue.

Before:

[10] → [20] → [30]
 ↑
Front

After Dequeue:

[20] → [30]
 ↑
Front

The element at the front is removed.

4.3 Peek / Front

Returns the front element without removing it.

Example:

[10] → [20] → [30]
 ↑
Front

Peek gives:

10

but 10 remains in the queue.

4.4 Rear / Back

Returns the last element of the queue without removing it.

[10] → [20] → [30]
              ↑
             Rear

Back gives:

30
4.5 IsEmpty

Checks whether the queue contains any elements.

A queue can be:

Empty

or

Non-empty
5. Queue Overflow

Overflow occurs when we try to insert an element into a queue that has reached its maximum capacity.

Example:

Queue size = 5

[10][20][30][40][50]

The queue is full.

Trying to insert:

60

causes overflow in a fixed-size implementation.

Remember

Insertion into a full queue → Overflow

6. Queue Underflow

Underflow occurs when we try to remove an element from an empty queue.

Queue:

[ ]

Trying to perform:

Dequeue

causes underflow.

Remember

Deletion from an empty queue → Underflow

7. Queue Representation

A Queue can be implemented using different data structures.

The major approaches are:

Queue
│
├── Array
│
├── Linked List
│
├── Circular Queue
│
